# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the X11 / Xft / fontconfig stack.
#
# These libraries allocate process-lifetime caches (atom tables, font pattern
# caches, config databases) that are never freed by design -- LSan reports them
# as leaks on every run. Suppressing them here keeps a real leak in wm2's own
# code visible instead of buried under third-party noise.
#
# REQUIRED companion setting (see tests/support/WmFixture.h::sanitizerEnv):
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=...:fast_unwind_on_malloc=0
# Without it the stripped system-library frames never appear in the recorded
# stack trace, so none of these patterns ever match and the suppressions are
# silently inert.

leak:libX11.so
leak:libXft.so
leak:libfontconfig
leak:FcFontSet
leak:FcPatternObject

# libXrandr leaks 32 bytes per connection when the extension is ABSENT.
#
# Added by plan 08-05, which introduced the only XRRQueryExtension call in the
# tree. Unlike the entries above -- process-lifetime caches that are never freed
# by design -- this one is a defect on the library's negative path: it allocates
# its per-display bookkeeping and then fails to register the close hook that
# would free it, so XCloseDisplay does not clean up.
#
# Not inferred from a stack trace. Reproduced standalone on this host with a
# twelve-line program that opens a display, calls XRRQueryExtension once and
# closes the display, with no wm2 code linked at all:
#
#   RANDR present  -> XRRQueryExtension returns 1, ASan clean
#   RANDR absent   -> XRRQueryExtension returns 0, 32 bytes leaked in 1 alloc,
#                     allocated at libXrandr.so.2+0x2a55 via XRRQueryExtension
#
# It therefore surfaces only in the [wm_norandr] group, where the server is
# started with `-extension RANDR`. Bounded at 32 bytes for the process's whole
# life, because the WM queries the extension exactly once at startup.
leak:libXrandr.so
